<DocsLayout
  title="Thread follow-ups"
  eyebrow="Agents & automation"
  description="Request one-shot reminders, recurring CI checks, and topic-triggered follow-ups in an existing Roder thread."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/agent-control/", title: "Agent control" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/persistent-agents/", title: "Persistent agents & issues" }}
>
  <p>
    Ask Roder to check CI periodically or revisit an operation after a delay. Thread
    events resume the existing conversation when it is idle, retaining its transcript,
    model, workspace, and tool restrictions. They do not hold a shell command or
    inference request open while waiting.
  </p>

  <h2>Request a concrete follow-up</h2>
  <p>
    For example: "Check this pull request every minute for the next hour. Investigate
    failing checks, but do not merge it. Stop checking when CI reaches a terminal state."
    Give the agent a target, permitted actions, and a stopping condition. Subscriptions
    should be created only when you request a follow-up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent uses <code>subscribe_event</code> with instructions for a later turn.
    This example requests a bounded recurring check; replace the pull request number
    and instructions with the actual task.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "topic": "ci:pr-167",
  "prompt": "Check PR 167 CI. Investigate failures within the requested scope. Do not merge. Cancel this subscription when checks are terminal and report the result.",
  "intervalSeconds": 60,
  "maxDeliveries": 30,
  "ttlSeconds": 3600
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Timers and published topics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Set <code>delaySeconds</code> without an interval for a one-shot reminder.</li>
    <li>Set <code>intervalSeconds</code> for recurring checks. An optional delay controls the first tick.</li>
    <li>Omit both to listen only for <code>publish_event</code> messages on the named topic.</li>
    <li>Use <code>list_event_subscriptions</code> to inspect next ticks, remaining deliveries, pending state, and delivery errors.</li>
    <li>Use <code>cancel_event_subscription</code> with the subscription ID to stop it and discard pending delivery. A turn already admitted continues.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A busy thread retains one pending delivery per subscription; ticks coalesce rather
    than starting overlapping turns. A published event counts queued subscriptions,
    not completed follow-ups. Topics are local to the runtime and require explicit
    subscribers.
  </p>

  <h2>Lifetime and authority</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Keep the owning runtime running.</strong> Subscriptions expire within
    24 hours and disappear when the harness exits. They do not survive restart or
    provide an unattended service while the desktop app is closed.
  </div>
  <p>
    A tick grants no new permissions. The resumed turn still follows policy, approval
    requirements, and the original task scope. Cancel recurring checks once their
    requested stopping condition is reached. Use separately configured durable
    automations for schedules that must survive process restarts; those create
    independent run threads rather than continuing this conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>App-server integration</h2>
  <p>
    Clients use <code>thread/events/subscribe</code>, <code>thread/events/list</code>,
    <code>thread/events/cancel</code>, and <code>thread/events/publish</code>.
    Subscription requests include <code>threadId</code>. The thread must have started
    a turn in this runtime so the harness has a real continuation context. A client
    disconnect does not remove a subscription if the owning runtime stays alive.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delay and interval values range from 1 to 86,400 seconds.
    <code>maxDeliveries</code> defaults to 100, with a maximum of 1,000;
    <code>ttlSeconds</code> defaults to 86,400 and cannot exceed that limit.
    A one-shot delay receives one delivery.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
